FLOW: recipe-card.qa.flow
DESCRIPTION: A kitchen-ready recipe card for a planned dinner, in the household's language
LAST UPDATED: 2026-10-20

NOTE: Cards are PNGs rendered by bin/recipe-card on the Bot's computer and attached to the reply.
Amounts come from what is actually in the basket / last order when nemlig is connected, otherwise from
typical Danish pack sizes scaled to the household.

================================================================================
SECTION 1: RECIPES
================================================================================

TEST: "Recipe card for a planned day — happy path"

PRECONDITIONS:
- A meal plan exists

STEP 1: "Ask"
WHEN I write "opskrift til tirsdag"
THEN I get a recipe card image for Tuesday's dinner
AND it shows the title, servings and total time, ingredients with amounts, equipment,
  and a step grid that shows which ingredients go into which step
AND times and temperatures are realistic (no "5 minutes" for something that takes 20)
AND servings match the household

RESULT: A card I can cook from on the phone or on the fridge.

================================================================================

TEST: "Recipe in English"

STEP 1: "Ask in English"
WHEN I write "recipe card for Tuesday, in English"
THEN the card's headings, units (tsp/tbsp), weekday and step texts are English
AND product names that come from nemlig stay as nemlig spells them

RESULT: Language follows the user.

================================================================================

TEST: "Recipe for something not on the plan"

STEP 1: "Ask"
WHEN I write "giv mig en opskrift på boller i karry"
THEN I get a recipe card for it
AND he asks whether to add the ingredients to the basket (he doesn't add them by himself)

RESULT: Ad-hoc recipes don't change the basket without a yes.

================================================================================

TEST: "Card can't be rendered"

PRECONDITIONS:
- Chrome on the Bot's computer is broken

STEP 1: "Ask"
WHEN I ask for a recipe card
THEN he runs install/doctor to repair it and retries
AND if it still fails he sends the recipe as plain text and says the card failed

RESULT: A broken renderer never blocks dinner.
